lude difficulty options  however some fans of the aptly named  souls-like genre disagree with that  assessment feeling strongly that the  games benefit from their cryptic design  choices and precise punishing combat  from software itself has long stood by  its stance too imploring all gamers to  embrace the challenge so that they can  feel an equal sense of reward by  overcoming it despite this speaking in  an interview with playstation blog  miyazaki made it clear that he believes  the conversation is still one worth  having he said quote it's a valid  discussion i feel like our approach to  these games not just eldon ring is to  design them to encourage the player to  overcome adversity we don't try to force  difficulty or make things hard for the  sake of it we want players to use their  cunning study the game memorize what's  happening and learn from their mistakes  miyazaki went on to share that he  believes the barrier of entry has been  lowered in elden ring as well due to  design choices that now provide  additional player agency and that he  hopes quote players embrace that idea of  receiving help from others he also  promised that the multiplayer function  of the game has been streamlined to put  an emphasis on working together to  overcome elton ring's toughest  challenges with this in mind miyazaki  feels confident that elton ring will  have the highest completion rate in from  software's souls-like library miyazaki's  interview also included confirmation  that eldon ring will feature a new game  plus mode and multiple endings two  components that have become a core part  of what makes from software games  endlessly replayable for die hard fans  the tech savvy killjoy a character in  riot games tactical shooter valorent was  recently shown in a tweet enjoying a  piece of nft art which raised some  eyebrows among more than a few fans riot  quickly deleted the tweet and has since  issued an apology the tweet was made by  the official german valorant twitter  account as a way to highlight killjoy's  hometown of berlin and spotlight  computer-generated art according to  riot's apology the team didn't realize  the artwork featured in the tweet by  artist martin hora was an nft the  statement tweeted out by the german  valerian twitter account reads quote  since killjoy loves programming we  wanted to introduce you to computer  generated art from around the world  however we were not aware that the  selected work was an nft in no way did  we intend to include nfts as a part of  killjoy's work and hobbies fans in the  responses to the clarification tweets  seem to be relieved that one of the  game's fan favorite characters was not  into nfts but it's currently unclear  what riot's overall stance on the  technology is if you want to know more  about nfts especially in games i suggest  you go check out my video on this  channel where i talk all about it  so how do you feel about this new  pokemon lore are you excited or against  elden ring being easy to beat let me  know in the comments and make sure to  like subscribe and come back next week  for more gamespot news have a great  weekend  [Music]
 

